The examples in this directory are described in the userguide.


Short description
-----------------

ale1:

 Viscoelastic problem:
 Oscillating Couette flow with a freely floating solid particle
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 second-order time integration
 ALE body-fitting mesh scheme
 Mesh generated by Gmsh using triangles

ale2:

 Viscoelastic problem:
 Oscillating Couette flow with a freely floating solid particle
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance.
 second-order time integration
 ALE body-fitting mesh scheme
 Mesh generated by Gmsh using triangles

ale3:

 Navier-Stokes problem:
 Oscillating Couette flow with a freely floating solid particle
 Neutrally buoyant particle ( density of particle = density of fluid )
 Second-order implicit Gear discretization. First step: Euler implicit.
 ALE body-fitting mesh scheme
 Particle updater uses a second-order AB2 scheme
 Mesh generated by Gmsh using triangles

ale4:

 Navier-Stokes problem:
 Oscillating Couette flow with a freely floating solid particle
 Neutrally buoyant particle ( density of particle = density of fluid )
 Second-order implicit Gear discretization. First step: Euler implicit.
 ALE body-fitting mesh scheme
 Particle updater uses a predictor/corrector scheme
 Mesh generated by Gmsh using triangles

ale5:

 Viscoelastic problem (Oldroyd-B):
 Couette flow with a freely floating solid particle
 Neutrally buoyant particle ( density of particle = density of fluid )
 Second-order Gear with Newton iteration for inertia terms.
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Semi-implicit Gear with velocity predicition for the CE.
 Second-order time integration
 ALE body-fitting mesh scheme
 Particle updater uses a second-order AB2 scheme
 Mesh generated by Gmsh using triangles
 This problem studies particle migration in a Couette flow.
 See Huang et al., J. Fluid Mech. (1997), vol. 343, pp. 73–94.

ale6:

 Viscoelastic problem (Oldroyd-B):
 Couette flow with a freely floating solid particle
 Neutrally buoyant particle ( density of particle = density of fluid )
 Second-order Gear with Newton iteration for inertia terms.
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance (stress implicit).
 Semi-implicit Gear with velocity prediction for the CE.
 Second-order time integration
 ALE body-fitting mesh scheme
 Particle updater uses a second-order AB2 scheme
 Mesh generated by Gmsh using triangles
 This problem studies particle migration in a Couette flow.
 See Huang et al., J. Fluid Mech. (1997), vol. 343, pp. 73–94.
 NOTE: this problem is similar to ale5, but now with the stress-implicit method

ale7:

 Viscoelastic problem (Oldroyd-B):
 Couette flow with a freely floating solid particle
 Neutrally buoyant particle ( density of particle = density of fluid )
 Second-order Gear with velocity prediction for inertia terms.
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance (stress implicit).
 Semi-implicit Gear with velocity prediction for the CE.
 Second-order time integration
 ALE body-fitting mesh scheme
 Particle updater uses a second-order AB2 scheme
 Mesh generated by Gmsh using triangles
 This problem studies particle migration in a Couette flow.
 See Huang et al., J. Fluid Mech. (1997), vol. 343, pp. 73–94.
 NOTE: this problem is similar to ale6, but now with the Newton iteration
 for the inertia terms replaced by a one-step prediction for the convection
 velocity.

ale8:

 Viscoelastic problem (Oldroyd-B):
 Couette flow with a freely floating solid particle
 Neutrally buoyant particle ( density of particle = density of fluid )
 Second-order Gear with velocity prediction for inertia terms.
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance (stress implicit).
 L2-projection of c=exp(s) on the finite element approximation space in the
 momentum balance.
 Semi-implicit Gear with velocity prediction for the CE.
 Second-order time integration
 ALE body-fitting mesh scheme
 Particle updater uses a second-order AB2 scheme
 Mesh generated by Gmsh using triangles
 This problem studies particle migration in a Couette flow.
 See Huang et al., J. Fluid Mech. (1997), vol. 343, pp. 73–94.
 NOTE: this problem is similar to ale7, but now using the L2-projection of
       c=exp(s). 

ale9:

 Stokes problem:
 Oscillating Couette flow with a freely floating solid particle.
 ALE body-fitting mesh scheme.
 Particle updater uses a second-order AB2 scheme.
 Mesh generated by Gmsh using triangles.
 A remeshing is performed every a fixed number of steps.

ale10:

 Stokes problem:
 Oscillating Couette flow with a freely floating solid particle.
 ALE body-fitting mesh scheme.
 Particle updater uses a second-order AB2 scheme.
 Mesh generated by Gmsh using triangles.
 A remeshing is performed when the maximum normalized area or aspect ratio 
 of the mesh elements is higher than a threshold.

ale11:

 Viscoelastic problem:
 Oscillating Couette flow with a freely floating solid particle
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Explicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance.
 Particle updater uses a second-order AB2 scheme.
 second-order time integration
 ALE body-fitting mesh scheme
 Periodic velocities/conformation tensor using collocation on curves
 Mesh generated by Gmsh using triangles
 Remeshing is performed when the maximum normalized area or aspect ratio 
 of the mesh elements is higher than a threshold.
 After remeshing, the field variables from previous time steps are obtained
 on the current mesh by solving a projection problem.
 Note: the coordinates of the old (discarded) mesh at previous time steps
 are projected onto the new (remeshed) mesh to obtain the new mesh at
 previous time steps. This is necessary to find the new mesh velocity.

ale12:

 Viscoelastic problem:
 Oscillating Couette flow with a freely floating solid particle
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance.
 Particle updater uses a second-order AB2 scheme.
 second-order time integration
 ALE body-fitting mesh scheme
 Periodic velocities/conformation tensor using collocation on curves
 Mesh generated by Gmsh using triangles
 Remeshing is performed when the maximum normalized area or aspect ratio 
 of the mesh elements is higher than a threshold.
 After remeshing, the field variables from previous time steps are obtained
 on the current mesh by solving a projection problem.
 Note: the coordinates of the old (discarded) mesh at previous time steps
 are projected onto the new (remeshed) mesh to obtain the new mesh at
 previous time steps. This is necessary to find the new mesh velocity.

ale13:

 Viscoelastic problem:
 Oscillating Couette flow with a freely floating solid particle
 DEVSS-G/SUPG
 Implicit bilinear terms of CE in momentum balance.
 Particle updater uses a second-order AB2 scheme.
 second-order time integration
 ALE body-fitting mesh scheme
 Periodic velocities/conformation tensor using collocation on surfaces
 Mesh generated by Gmsh using tetrahedrons
 Remeshing is performed when the maximum normalized volume or aspect ratio 
 of the mesh elements is higher than a threshold.
 After remeshing, the field variables from previous time steps are obtained
 on the current mesh by solving a projection problem.
 Note: the coordinates of the old (discarded) mesh at previous time steps
 are projected onto the new (remeshed) mesh to obtain the new mesh at
 previous time steps. This is necessary to find the new mesh velocity.
 Note2: if the log transformation method is used, a projection problem
 is solved at each time step to find the L2-projection of c=exp(s). 
 NOTE: This problem is similar to ale12, but now in 3D

ale14:

 Viscoelastic problem:
 Oscillating Couette flow with a freely floating solid particle
 This example is similar to ale13, but now using sparskit as a solver and metis
 to renumber the mesh
